  "limits": "# Original linked-reader physical selector\n\nPostgreSQL 17 uses the original two table schemas and all 13 constraints, unchanged 0c3 linked-reader SQL, and 6,000 synthetic complete proofs of at least 64,820 bytes each. The HASH predicate reads only a non-NULL union key, which is implied by the original strict union equality. The original proof checksum predicate remains in the reader. The obsolete global partial proof-hand index is retired after the replacement selector is valid; original unique hand-id and primary-key indexes remain.\n\nNative qualification compares the complete original four-field result bag and actual custom/generic plans, refusing any global old-proof index scan or link-table sequential scan. It measures 6,000 instrumented proof serializations for the old build and zero for the exact new candidate build, without claiming production duration. Instrumented timings include counter overhead. Valid 16KB union text, large proofs, NULL-union semantics, original hand-id lookup, snapshot concurrency and all original constraints are exercised.\n\nA genuine interrupted local CONCURRENTLY build supplies the failed index catalog. The exact recovery module and commands execute against real PostgreSQL: invalid original selector is dropped, new selector is built and proven valid, then the old valid proof-hand index is retired. Only the two fixture index OID constants are mapped; production remains bound to failed OID 275975891 and retired OID 271236984. Fresh ownership, exact definitions, state, no constraints/builders/incoming references, five automatic column dependencies, full table constraints and surviving-index inventory are required. Both drops and build share 600 seconds in the maintained applier; there is no automatic retry.\n\nSource65/generation, freeze and maintenance callbacks are controlled local fixture owners, not production certificates. Production requires protected-main installation, exact catalog/history readback, unchanged actual 13/20 owner metadata and source65/generation, and the original book closure. Installed 65332 history and bytes remain immutable; its old physical index is explicitly replaced, rather than pretending it remains present.\n\nNormal clean application binds its own newly validated selector OID and performs the guarded retirement last. If the selector is already valid after an interrupted operation, the explicit `recover_index=public.union_pnl_cash_links_valid_proof_hand`, `recover_oid=271236984`, `recover_selector_oid=<fresh observed valid selector OID>` and original observation timestamp select retirement-only continuation. It refuses selector replacement/drop paths and skips CCI entirely. Dry-run performs only catalog/window/snapshot reads and exits before either DROP. No unknown outcome initiates a retry.\n\nIf retirement completed but pure qualification or atomic history failed, fresh exact selector shape/OID, remaining constraints and index inventory admit only the original pure qualification and same-byte atomic history. Old-index absence never triggers a DROP or CCI replay. Both normal and explicit continuation validate this completed physical state; dry-run remains read-only. Native fault cases prove failed qualification, failed history and successful history-only continuation on real PostgreSQL.\n"
